  "slug": "guided-novo-ligandforge-9226",
  "summary": "Benchmarks on DNA-encoded libraries, kinase bioactivity, and drug-blind cancer response show held-out performance depends on split design and dataset.",
  "synthesis": "The shared thread is evaluation under distribution shift: how well learned or physics-based models transfer to chemical matter, targets, or drugs absent from training. The DNA-encoded library study finds that out-of-distribution hit discrimination is target-dependent across ML, docking, and co-folding approaches. The kinase benchmark compares seven architectures on 352,874 ChEMBL records across 507 targets under random, scaffold, and target-held-out splits. A molecular ML study links CKA representational alignment to performance gaps, proves an upper bound for linear regression, and introduces MMCD. The drug-blind cancer response study finds generalization occurs only through shared mechanisms and argues that drug-blind benchmark outcomes reflect the dataset rather than the model. The new element is the explicit tie between representational alignment and performance gaps, together with a dataset-level reading of split-based benchmarks.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-04-23"
}
